WEBVTT

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.200
pressure on islamic state as iraqi special forces enter the city limits in

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.340
mosul for the first time in more than two years

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.810
troops say they are facing fierce resistance from is rocket-propelled

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:19.280
grenades mortars and machine guns we'll have

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.800
the latest from our correspondent who's traveling alongside iraqi troops

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.960
also this lunchtime making britain's cyberspace secure

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.000
the government unveils its 1.9 billion pound plan to defeat

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:31.920
defeat the hackers.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.800
Into the last week, the final act in one of the most bitter presidential campaigns in

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:38.120
American history.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:42.080
Campaigners consider seeking a judicial review into the decision not to hold a public inquiry

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.240
into the so-called Battle of Orgreave.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:49.140
England and Scotland players are told they can't wear poppies when they play on Armistice

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.710
Day because FIFA says it's a political symbol.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.980
In the south stripped of its contract, the controversial patient transport service which

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.000
faced complaints from the start.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:05.230
And rising from the ashes, a temporary school opens to students after their old classrooms

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:22.910
burn down.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.890
Good afternoon and welcome to the BBC News at 1.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:30.000
Iraqi forces have entered the eastern outskirts of Mosul for the first time since

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.270
they began their military campaign to recapture the city from the islamic state group more than

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.490
a million civilians are thought to be trapped inside the assault now in its third week involves

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:45.610
hundreds of troops in heavily armored vehicles together with tanks and bulldozers they're now

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:50.650
believed to be in the eastern perimeter of the city kakajili it's still reported that they've

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:55.830
retaken control of the state television building our correspondent ian panel is embedded with the

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:00.000
the Iraqi special forces on the front line he says the Islamic State fighters

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:09.090
are putting up fierce resistance. Certainly this morning as we move closer

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:14.200
around through the desert they called in coalition airstrikes to a number of

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:20.530
different targets I think sort of four or five. The point that they chose to come

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.320
in on this morning they've met much stiffer resistance than eyewitness that

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.000
they've had to face over the last few days. There's been sustained small arms fire

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.200
rocket-copel grenades, mortars, suspected IEDs, improvised explosive devices,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.690
and they've also had to call in some coalition air support.

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.810
I mean, this is a unit that's had the most momentum, the most confidence,

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:47.010
and the most experienced, because, of course, it's fought in Ramadi and Fallujah,

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:52.440
two other Islamic State strongholds, which they managed to get back in the hands of the Iraqi government.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.520
So they're experienced, they're confident, but the truth is it's heavy-going.

00:02:56.740 --> 00:03:00.000
This is ground zero, really, for the Islamic State.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.350
Nobody expects them to turn and run, or certainly not en masse.

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:14.450
Many of them are willing to stand, fight, and die, and that makes for a very tough opponent.

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:21.520
They're getting in, if they get in today, that will be a story, that will be a moment.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:26.820
But it's likely to take weeks, if not months, to actually clear the city completely.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.540
And even then, many think the battle will just shift.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.420
The Iraqi Prime Minister Haider al-Abadi has appeared on state television wearing combat

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:44.520
fatigues, urging Islamic State Group militants in Mosul to surrender.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.760
We are trying to close off all escape for Daesh, and God willing, we will chop off the

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.760
snake's head.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.630
I tell all the people of Nineveh, and the people of Mosul in particular, that those

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.370
heroes will chop off Daesh's head.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.930
We will enclose them.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.000
They will have no escape.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.000
They either die or surrender.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Oh, yes, that's the one.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.170
Here, the Chancellor, Philip Hammond,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.170
says the UK will strike back if it comes under cyber attack.

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.170
He's announcing a package of measures designed

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.170
to protect the government, businesses and people

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.170
from online threats, including from state-sponsored hackers.

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:21.060
The Kremlin has dismissed allegations by the head of MI5

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.060
that Russia is mounting cyber attacks and other aggressive measures

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.060
which pose a growing threat to Britain.

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:29.060
Our technology correspondent, Rory Kethen-Jones, reports.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.710
We know our computers are under threat, we're getting used to the idea that our phones might

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:41.330
not be secure, but surely we don't have to worry about the kettle being hacked.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.510
Well yes, in a world where more and more devices are being connected to the internet, we're

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.360
all facing new threats.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.850
This home security camera set up is one example.

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.270
It punches a hole through your firewall.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:58.820
This kind of device was hijacked in a recent major attack, a taste of what's to come.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.000
We keep buying connected devices.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.960
to put on our network, whether it's a smart thermostat

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.450
or a smart kettle or whatever, and that's great,

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.350
but we think about all the personal information

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.040
that's on those devices, but it gets worse.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.080
These things are so insecure,

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.250
they're starting to be turned against us.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.020
They're starting to be used to attack parts of the internet,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.000
to take down critical infrastructures.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.340
- It's this kind of danger

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.100
that the government's new cybersecurity strategy

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.900
is designed to confront.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.430
The Chancellor promised action to defend against cyber attackers

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.000
and fight back if necessary.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.010
"The public have much to gain from active cyber defence and with the proper safeguards

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:44.450
in place to protect privacy, these measures have the potential to be transformational

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:52.470
in ensuring that UK internet users are secure by default."

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:56.780
However much we spend on shoring up Britain's defences against the hackers, we're going

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.000
to need a lot more people with cyber security skills.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:03.300
and right now there's a shortage of that kind of know-how.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:07.910
What we're basically looking at is we're looking for suspicious activity from an IP address.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:14.010
At BT's headquarters, cyber security apprentices are hearing about the company's own security tools.

00:06:14.010 --> 00:06:18.410
The government wants more young people to see this as a valuable career.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.510
It's a different attack every day, a different threat every day,

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:23.920
and it's just, it's always keeping you on your toes and you're learning new things.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.910
So it's just very exciting to be in.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:29.570
Cyber security is an upcoming field. It's going to be very important.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.790
pretty much forever as long as we use computers, it's a very fast paced industry.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:39.470
More money will now be spent on cyber crime investigators, but keeping up with the threat

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:45.090
from hackers ranging from teenagers to nation states will be a major challenge.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:48.630
Rory Catlin-Jones, BBC News.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:52.630
One of the most bitter US presidential election campaigns in living memory is entering its

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.930
final week.

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:57.720
The Republican contender Donald Trump, who's been boosted by the announcement of a fresh

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.000
FBI investigation into his rival's emails,

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:02.870
is targeting previously safe democratic states.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:05.920
Hillary Clinton, who had been riding high in the opinion polls,

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.600
is continuing to level her attacks at Mr Trump's fitness for office.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:12.240
Our correspondent Gary O'Donoghue sent this report.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:16.150
The final push by the two candidates,

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:18.730
putting themselves through a punishing schedule,

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.810
crisscrossing this country, fighting for every last vote.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:27.600
Just a few days ago, Hillary Clinton appeared headed towards victory.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:30.000
But the discovery of new emails,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.780
by the FBI, an issue

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.780
that's dogged her for three years

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:35.360
has put her in conflict

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.650
with the country's top law enforcement

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:39.660
agency. I'm not making

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.800
excuses. I've said it was a mistake

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.610
and I regret it and now

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.690
they apparently want to look at emails

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.370
of one of my staffers

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.610
and I am sure they will reach

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:51.610
the same conclusion they

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.470
did when they looked at my

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:55.830
emails for the last year. There is

00:07:55.830 --> 00:07:57.690
no case here.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:00.000
Hillary Clinton has

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:06.200
He vowed not to allow the email saga to distract her from this election, and indeed the FBI

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:12.660
was booed here at this rally, but the investigators are the last people you want to take on in

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:19.970
the run-up to polling day, and of course, for a real opponent, it's a golden opportunity.

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:25.210
And Donald Trump has wasted no time seizing on the revelations, no longer talking so much

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:30.000
about a rigged election, but more about how proud he is of the FBI for

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.420
having released its news so close to polling day.

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.540
I have to give the FBI credit.

00:08:35.540 --> 00:08:42.750
That was so bad what happened originally, and it took guts for director Comey to make the

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:48.120
move that he made in light of the kind of opposition he had where they're trying to

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:52.510
protect her from criminal prosecution.

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:57.870
As she headed for another day of campaigning in the battleground state of Florida, Hillary

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.000
Clinton knows the fresh FBI.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.760
probe is unlikely to be finished before polling day it's now a battle for public

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:11.090
opinion and the big question what will the undecided voters make of these new

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:19.610
revelations Gary O'Donoghue BBC News here campaigners have vowed to carry on

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.990
fighting for a public inquiry into the 1984 clashes between striking miners and

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:28.190
police at the Orgreave coking plant in South Yorkshire saying the gloves are now

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:30.000
off yesterday the home secretary

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.800
Amber Rudd ruled out an investigation. Danny Savage is in Barnsley for us so

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:41.330
that the Battle of Orgreave goes on. Yes Simon, the people say their campaign is

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:45.930
not going to be derailed by the announcement yesterday. The indication

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:49.250
from the government up until yesterday for the last few weeks is that there was

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:53.220
going to be some sort of inquiry into what happened at Orgreave but then the

00:09:53.220 --> 00:09:56.220
announcement came that there wasn't going to be any official investigation at

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:00.000
all. People here were devastated and here at the National Union of

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.860
mine workers' headquarters in Barnsley, there was a public meeting and news conference this

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:10.200
morning where that defiant message was put out. They simply reject the government's arguments

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:15.000
that it is not in the public interest to hold some sort of investigation. And there

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.620
was a QC here, a barrister as well, Mark George QC. He said that in the wake of the

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:26.760
miners being arrested back on that day in 1984, court cases followed. And he said perjury

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:29.920
was committed in Sheffield Crown Court by police officers.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.620
officers. If that's not a miscarriage of justice, I don't know what is. So his message

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:40.180
was that there have been problems at the time. They must be fully investigated. They

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.420
must be publicly investigated to see where this investigation goes from here. But the

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:48.800
people here, the message is simple. They are not going to give up. And all these banners

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.360
around here at the miners' headquarters often depict a campaign or a cause that mine workers

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:58.460
have fought for over the years. And that's what Allgrieve is about to them now.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.000
you very much researchers are recruiting for the biggest ever clinical trial on

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.260
the best way to treat chronic fatigue syndrome also known as me it's thought

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.920
to affect 150 children in England and can lead to mental health problems and

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.180
missing school more than 700 children and young people will take part in this

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.540
trial and they would have to have talking therapy sessions using the

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:24.900
internet so they don't have to travel for treatment our health correspondent

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:30.000
Robert Pigott reports Lizzie Hawken describes chronic

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.560
fatigue syndrome as awful, a life of debilitating fatigue, brain fog and pain.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:41.360
CFS is diagnosed in children if they have such symptoms, usually following an infection,

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.620
and they persist for more than three months.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:50.250
I was suffering with kind of crippling headaches, pins and needles feeling all over my body,

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:56.890
muscle cramps, spasms, aching, so much more than that idea of just tiredness.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:00.000
It really affected my whole body on a really biological level.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.700
Chronic fatigue syndrome is thought to affect one in 50 young people.

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:10.570
One in 100 are badly enough affected to be off school at least a day a week.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:13.910
But researchers say a new talking therapy offers hope.

00:12:13.910 --> 00:12:19.370
63% of those who received it in a trial in Holland recovered after six months, compared

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:24.350
with only 8% of those getting standard treatment.

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:30.000
Now more than 700 young people with CFS are being recruited to test the talking therapy.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.730
"Children access the treatment through a platform called FitNet, and that has a lot

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:37.150
of information for them.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:40.150
They keep diaries about their sleep and their activity because that's what we're trying

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:41.150
to change.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.870
And then every week they have a consultation with a therapist and they have that consultation

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:45.880
online.

00:12:45.880 --> 00:12:49.260
So they get individualised treatment, advice and support.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.960
They do a lot of work on their own to try and sort out what they're going to be doing,

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:56.060
and then they get supported by a specialist therapist.

00:12:56.060 --> 00:13:00.000
The really interesting thing about FitNet is that parents are also getting a lot of

00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:05.050
An estimated 40,000 young people have CFS,

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:09.740
but campaigners claim the vast majority are getting too little help.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:14.580
At the moment, only one in ten of those children can access specialist treatment.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:19.100
This is a way to reach out to them and ensure that more children can be treated,

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:24.360
because at the moment, with the NHS cuts, we're losing services, not gaining them.

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:30.000
Researchers say psychological therapy can be as potent as a drug in treating CFS.

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:34.540
They hope to show it can save enough money to make it available to young patients throughout

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:35.480
England.

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:39.010
Robert Piggott, BBC News.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:42.710
One of England's leading teaching hospitals is expected to be put in special measures

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:43.710
today.

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:47.890
It's after inspectors rated St George's in south-west London as inadequate.

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:52.170
The Trust Thoreau says it's made real progress since the inspection in June.

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:55.510
Our health correspondent Dominic Hughes has more.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:14:00.000
St George's in Tooting in south-west London is one of England's leading teaching hospitals.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.960
But a report from the Care Quality Commission describes a hospital with poorly maintained buildings,

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.540
leading to serious concerns around the safety of patients.

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:11.410
Some of the clinical areas had water coming in through the roof.

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:13.780
There were problems with the water supply.

00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:18.760
It was contaminated and they weren't taking steps to maintain a safe water supply.

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.500
Some of the operating theatres were not fit for purpose.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:27.020
NHS trusts are recommended for special measures when inspectors from the Care Quality Commission

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:30.000
rate the organisation as inadequate in terms

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.450
of leadership and one other key area such as being safe effective caring or responsive another body

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:41.330
nhs improvement normally then steps in with a much more hands-on approach to the running of

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:47.940
the trust st george's will become the 18th trust in england to now be in special measures some of

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:51.460
the reason for these trusts going into special measures is undoubtedly due to the fact that the

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:57.950
financial pressure is is racking up on nhs trust but so also is their difficulty in recruiting the

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.000
the numbers of staff that they need to

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.000
meet the standards.

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.910
It is possible to turn performance around.

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:08.220
13 hospital trusts, including the one that runs this hospital, the Royal Blackburn in

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:12.560
Lancashire, have been through the process and are now out of special measures.

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:18.100
But it did mean staff had to really focus on patient care.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:22.060
Special measures was a shock to the trust, but it led to a valuable rethink about the

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.140
way the whole organisation worked.

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:29.860
We've made improvements right the way through the organisation and really everything starts

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:33.780
with staff if you have the right staff engagement that means you can make the

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:37.720
right changes and you can improve quality and compassion. So everybody's been very

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:42.530
kind? Everybody. The experience here is that by concentrating on issues like the

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:47.630
care of frail elderly patients the whole trust has improved. When we speak to the

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:51.710
staff they say yes in some respects it was the best thing that ever happened to

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:56.350
this trust. We now know our story and we've now managed to improve things and

00:15:56.350 --> 00:16:00.000
and we've got a better CQC rating and better harm reduction rating.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:04.520
But with a growing number of trusts in England facing real problems,

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:07.160
clinical and financial, as we head into winter,

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:10.180
there are concerns over the resilience of the health service.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:12.000
Dominic Hughes, BBC News.

00:16:12.710 --> 00:16:15.450
It's just after quarter past one, our top story this lunchtime.

